begin model

#@title: |
#  Noise-resistant circadian oscillator with sub-single-copy transcripts
#  (Vilar et al., 2002)

#@description: |
#  Variant of noise_resistant_circadian_oscillator_vilar2002 that differs in
#  one respect: both translation rates and both transcript degradation rates
#  are multiplied by one hundred, so beta_A = 5000 /h, beta_R = 500 /h,
#  delta_MA = 1000 /h and delta_MR = 50 /h. Every other reaction, rate constant
#  and initial count is identical to the primary model.
#  Scaling translation and transcript turnover by the same factor leaves the
#  protein yield per transcript unchanged, and the paper states that this has
#  no effect on protein dynamics in the deterministic limit. That statement is
#  made of the two-variable reduction of Eq. 2, in which mRNA has been
#  adiabatically eliminated, and it does not carry over unchanged to the full
#  Eq. 1 network simulated here, where each transcript is still a dynamical
#  variable with its own lifetime. Shortening that lifetime a hundredfold
#  shortens the deterministic period from 25.6 h to 21.6 h and lowers the
#  repressor peak from about 1,700 to about 1,250 molecules. The cycle is
#  moved, not destroyed.
#  What the rescaling does do, and what the figure is for, is drive the
#  transcript counts below one molecule on time average: both mRNAs now sit at
#  zero most of the time and appear in brief one- or two-copy spikes. The
#  protein oscillation continues through this, which is the sharpest statement
#  of the paper's thesis and the one made in its abstract — oscillations can be
#  present even when the time average of the number of mRNA molecules goes
#  below one. mRNA is an intermediate that does not drive the two slow
#  variables, so its fluctuations are averaged away rather than propagated.

#@keyword: |
#  circadian rhythm, gene regulation, genetic oscillator, low copy number,
#  transcriptional bursting, noise resistance, intrinsic noise, timescale
#  separation, protein sequestration, mass action, Gillespie algorithm, generic

#@reference: |
#  Vilar JMG, Kueh HY, Barkai N, Leibler S (2002). Mechanisms of
#  noise-resistance in genetic oscillators. Proceedings of the National Academy
#  of Sciences USA 99:5988-5992. doi:10.1073/pnas.092133899

#@note: |
#  Population-based model: every amount is a number of molecules and every rate
#  constant is per molecule per hour or per hour, exactly as tabulated in the
#  Fig. 1 caption with the four rates the Fig. 7 caption rescales. The paper
#  sets the cellular volume to unity "so that concentrations and number of
#  molecules are equivalent", so no conversion through NA and a volume is
#  applied anywhere; NA and V_ref appear in the parameters block as bookkeeping
#  only and do not enter the dynamics. Time is in hours throughout.

#@note: |
#  Notation map to Vilar et al. (2002). Da(a) is D_A and Da(a!+) is D_A';
#  Dr(a) is D_R and Dr(a!+) is D_R'; Ma() is M_A and Mr() is M_R; A(s) is the
#  free activator A, R(a) is the free repressor R, and A(s!1).R(a!1) is the
#  inactive complex C.

#@note: |
#  The activator carries a single binding site s, which binds either promoter
#  or the repressor. One site rather than two is what makes the generated
#  network the paper's nine species exactly: Eq. 1 has no species in which A is
#  bound to DNA and to R at the same time, and giving A two independent sites
#  would generate four such species that the published model does not contain.

#@note: |
#  delta_A appears twice, on two different rules, and this is deliberate — the
#  paper says so explicitly in the Fig. 1 caption. Free A degrades at delta_A,
#  and the complex C decays at delta_A because it is the activator inside it
#  that degrades, leaving free R behind. Activator bound to a promoter does not
#  degrade at all, and repressor inside the complex does not degrade at
#  delta_R.

begin parameters
  # Population-conversion constants and bookkeeping reactor volume
  NA     6.02214076e23  # molecules/mol
  V_ref  1.0            # L/reactor

  # Transcription rates from unbound and activator-bound promoters;
  # Fig. 1 caption in Vilar et al. (2002), unchanged by the Fig. 7 rescaling
  alpha_A      50     # /h
  alpha_A_act  500    # /h
  alpha_R      0.01   # /h
  alpha_R_act  50     # /h

  # Translation rates, one hundred times the Fig. 1 caption values;
  # Fig. 7 caption
  beta_A  5000  # /h
  beta_R  500   # /h

  # Transcript degradation rates, one hundred times the Fig. 1 caption values;
  # Fig. 7 caption. Protein degradation is unchanged.
  delta_MA  1000  # /h
  delta_MR  50    # /h
  delta_A   1     # /h
  delta_R   0.2   # /h

  # Binding of the activator to the two promoters and to the repressor
  gamma_A  1  # /(molecule*h)
  gamma_R  1  # /(molecule*h)
  gamma_C  2  # /(molecule*h)

  # Unbinding of the activator from the two promoters
  theta_A  50   # /h
  theta_R  100  # /h

  # Single-copy genes and an otherwise empty cell; Fig. 1 caption
  Da_init  1  # molecules
  Dr_init  1  # molecules
end parameters

begin molecule types
  # Activator gene promoter; site a binds one activator protein
  Da(a)
  # Repressor gene promoter; site a binds one activator protein
  Dr(a)
  # Activator mRNA
  Ma()
  # Repressor mRNA
  Mr()
  # Activator protein; the single site s binds a promoter or the repressor,
  # never both at once
  A(s)
  # Repressor protein; site a binds one activator protein
  R(a)
end molecule types

begin seed species
  # One copy of each gene, both promoters unbound, and no transcript, protein
  # or complex present at t = 0
  Da(a)  Da_init  # molecules
  Dr(a)  Dr_init  # molecules
end seed species

begin observables
  # Free activator and free repressor protein; panels a and c of Fig. 7
  Molecules Obs_A  A(s)
  Molecules Obs_R  R(a)

  # Inactive activator-repressor complex
  Molecules Obs_C  A(s!1).R(a!1)

  # Transcript counts; panels b and d of Fig. 7, both below one on time average
  Molecules Obs_Ma  Ma()
  Molecules Obs_Mr  Mr()

  # Promoter occupancy; D_A' and D_R', each zero or one
  Molecules Obs_Da_bound  Da(a!+)
  Molecules Obs_Dr_bound  Dr(a!+)

  # Activator in every form, free plus promoter-bound plus sequestered
  Molecules Obs_A_tot  A()
end observables

begin reaction rules
  # Reversible binding of the activator to its own promoter, converting
  # D_A into D_A'
  R_bind_Da: A(s) + Da(a) <-> A(s!1).Da(a!1)  gamma_A, theta_A

  # Reversible binding of the activator to the repressor promoter, converting
  # D_R into D_R'
  R_bind_Dr: A(s) + Dr(a) <-> A(s!1).Dr(a!1)  gamma_R, theta_R

  # Basal and activated transcription of the activator gene
  R_transcribe_A_basal: Da(a) -> Da(a) + Ma()  alpha_A
  R_transcribe_A_act: Da(a!+) -> Da(a!+) + Ma()  alpha_A_act

  # Basal and activated transcription of the repressor gene
  R_transcribe_R_basal: Dr(a) -> Dr(a) + Mr()  alpha_R
  R_transcribe_R_act: Dr(a!+) -> Dr(a!+) + Mr()  alpha_R_act

  # Translation of both transcripts
  R_translate_A: Ma() -> Ma() + A(s)  beta_A
  R_translate_R: Mr() -> Mr() + R(a)  beta_R

  # Spontaneous degradation of both transcripts
  R_degrade_Ma: Ma() -> 0  delta_MA
  R_degrade_Mr: Mr() -> 0  delta_MR

  # Spontaneous degradation of free activator and free repressor. The patterns
  # match the unbound proteins only: promoter-bound activator does not degrade,
  # and repressor inside the complex does not degrade at delta_R.
  R_degrade_A: A(s) -> 0  delta_A
  R_degrade_R: R(a) -> 0  delta_R

  # Irreversible sequestration of the activator by the repressor into the
  # inactive complex C; Eq. 1 gives the complex no dissociation route
  R_sequester: A(s) + R(a) -> A(s!1).R(a!1)  gamma_C

  # The complex decays by degradation of the activator inside it, releasing
  # free repressor. DeleteMolecules removes the activator alone rather than the
  # whole complex.
  R_degrade_A_in_C: A(s!1).R(a!1) -> R(a)  delta_A  DeleteMolecules
end reaction rules

end model

begin actions
  generate_network({overwrite=>1})  # 9 species, 16 reactions
  saveConcentrations()

  # Deterministic limit cycle, unchanged by the rescaling

  #@protocol: |
  #  Integrate the reaction rate equations of Eq. 1 with the Fig. 7 rates from
  #  the empty initial state over t = 0..400 h, sampling every 0.1 h. The
  #  protein limit cycle persists but is not identical to the primary model's:
  #  the period falls from 25.6 h to 21.6 h and the repressor peak from 1,717
  #  to 1,251 molecules. The invariance the paper reports for this rescaling
  #  holds in the two-variable reduction of Eq. 2, not in the full network.
  #  Transcript levels are one hundredth of the primary model's.

  #@figure: Fig. 7 in Vilar et al. (2002), deterministic counterpart

  resetConcentrations()
  simulate({method=>"ode",suffix=>"ode",t_start=>0,t_end=>400,n_steps=>4000,\
    atol=>1e-10,rtol=>1e-10})

  # Protein oscillation at sub-single-copy transcript numbers

  #@protocol: |
  #  Simulate one seeded Gillespie trajectory of the same network and the same
  #  initial state over t = 0..400 h, sampling every 0.1 h. Both transcript
  #  counts spend most of their time at zero, averaging about 0.14 and 0.17
  #  molecules, and reach one to five copies in brief spikes, while free
  #  activator and free repressor go on oscillating at the shortened period of
  #  this parameterization. The seed makes the committed reference output
  #  reproducible; verify_vilar2002.ipynb uses an ensemble rather than this one
  #  realization.

  #@figure: Fig. 7 in Vilar et al. (2002)

  resetConcentrations()
  simulate({method=>"ssa",suffix=>"ssa",t_start=>0,t_end=>400,n_steps=>4000,\
    seed=>92133899})

  # Partial-scaling SSA alternative
#  resetConcentrations()
#  simulate({method=>"ssa",poplevel=>100,suffix=>"psa",t_start=>0,t_end=>400,\
#    n_steps=>4000,seed=>92133899})

  # Network-free SSA alternative
#  resetConcentrations()
#  simulate({method=>"nf",suffix=>"nfr",t_start=>0,t_end=>400,n_steps=>4000,\
#    seed=>92133899,complex=>1})
end actions
